NOT COVERED BY THE LICENCE ABOVE

The licence covers the code and documentation written for this project. Two
things in this repository are not the author's to license, and are listed here
rather than left to be discovered.

1. The fault descriptions.

   `FAULT_TEXT_EXTRACTED` in `web/js/protocol.js` holds 374 short fault
   descriptions read out of the constant tables of PA-Soft's BMW Scanner 1.4.0
   software. They are the vendor's wording, not ours, and the MIT grant above
   does not extend to them. They are included because a fault code without its
   description is of no use to the person reading it, and because inventing
   replacements would mean shipping plausible-sounding guesses about faults on
   somebody's car — which this project refuses to do everywhere else.

   `tools/fault_text.py` is the extractor, and it is ours. Anyone who would
   rather not rely on the included copy can run it against their own copy of the
   vendor software.

   If the rights holder objects, write an issue or mail the address in the
   commit history and they will be removed.

2. Nothing else from the vendor.

   No vendor binary is in this repository. The protected executable, its memory
   dump and the module EEPROM images are all `.gitignore`d, deliberately and
   permanently. The protocol itself was recovered by observation, and a protocol
   is not anyone's property.

ON SAFETY

The warranty disclaimer above is not boilerplate here. This software talks to
the control modules of a motor vehicle and can erase their fault memories.
Erasing a fault memory destroys evidence a mechanic may need, and a vehicle that
reports no faults is not the same as a vehicle that has none. Use it on a car
you are responsible for, and read `README.md` on what it deliberately does not
do.
